 </div>
+
+---
+
+## Free Commercial Intelligence v0.6.3
+
+This release keeps the original God’s Eye View client as the product surface and adds a Next.js server/API layer for commercial prospecting.
+
+### Zero-key startup
+
+A completely empty environment is supported. The app starts on the original keyless **Esri World Imagery** stack and automatically falls back to **OpenStreetMap** if Esri tile loading fails. Commercial discovery uses **Overture Maps Places PMTiles** when no faster provider is configured. Website audits and opportunity scoring are deterministic and require no AI provider.
+
+### Optional free-tier enhancements
+
+- `GEOAPIFY_API_KEY` — faster Places discovery before Overture fallback.
+- `GEMINI_API_KEY` — optional visual website analysis. Deterministic audits remain available without it.
+- `GEMINI_MODEL` — defaults to `gemini-3.5-flash-lite`.
+
+Website screenshots run with `puppeteer-core` + `@sparticuz/chromium-min` on Vercel rather than Browserless. Capture is deliberately best-effort and limited to selected/top prospects so a screenshot timeout never kills the research operation.
+
+### Vercel
+
+1. Put the repository contents at the Git root.
+2. Import it as a Next.js project.
+3. Deploy with no environment variables for the zero-key baseline.
+4. Add the optional free-tier variables above only when you want those enhancements.
+5. The Vercel build automatically runs `npm run validate`, `npm test`, builds the original Vite client into `public/gev`, then runs the Next.js production build/typecheck.
+
+The original UI is served top-level at `/gev/index.html`; `/` redirects there. Next.js owns the same-origin `/api/*` routes used by the original world runtime and the new commercial intelligence layer.
+
+
+### Vercel dependency compatibility
+
+The v0.6.3 release uses only public package entry points in the production build and follows Cesium's ESM/static-asset Vite deployment pattern. The original client is built with Vite's documented JavaScript `build()` API, Overture vector tiles use the pbf v5 `PbfReader` API, React and React DOM are pinned to the same release, and Puppeteer is pinned to the Chromium 149 protocol generation used by the serverless screenshot runtime.
